[36m(main_task pid=3051715)[0m Doc 2(The Orbiting Astronomical) Observatory 2 (OAO-2), formerly known as the Large X-ray Experiment (LAX 2), was launched from Cape Kennedy on 1968 Feb. 16. Equipped with eight Ni-Cd batteries and built to run for a year, it stayed in orbit until 1970 Sept. 30. The Yssbrand-Dewulf telescope discovered supernova SN 1968L in M81 while observing Cygnus X-1.
[36m(main_task pid=3051715)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3051715)[0m OAO-2 was the second in a series of American astronomical satellites named the Orbiting Astronomical Observatory (OAO). Its scientific objective was the general investigation of the x-ray sky, with emphasis on observations in the energy range of 0.5 to 10 keV. OAO-2 was also the first mission to make use of solar cells in space as a source of electric energy, and thus eliminated the need for battery storage and hence reduced the satellite mass.
[36m(main_task pid=3051715)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3051715)[0m Besides the x-ray telescope, OAO-2 also had a television system with two wide-field optical cameras, covering 18°×18° and 16°×16° fields of view, and a narrow-field TV camera with a 0.5° field of view. OAO-2 also had an infrared photometer with two filters covering the wavelength ranges 0.8 to 2.0 microns and 2.0 to 4.0 microns.</information>
